heir hand, and keeping my dominant pairs to sweep in later. Plus, it's what I am supposed to do.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: I hold the Rocket (Black Joker + Red Joker) and a strong set of pairs (10s, Js, Ks) along with singles (3, 8, Q). My Teammate has only 3 cards remaining, while the Landlord has 10. Card counting indicates the opponents have no Queens (count is 0) and no pairs higher than Kings (no AA or 22 possible). This makes my pairs of 10s, Js, and Ks dominant. I have absolute control of the game with the Rocket. The goal is to maximize the chance of my teammate going out or to clear my own hand efficiently.\n\nReason: Playing the single ♠3 is the best move for the following reasons:\n1.  **Cooperation**: My teammate has only 3 cards. Leading the lowest single gives them the best opportunity to either play a low card they are stuck with or use a high card (like the remaining 2) to take the lead and finish.\n2.  **Provocation**: Leading a small single forces the Landlord to respond. If the Landlord plays a high card (A or 2) to win the trick, I can choose to use my Rocket to shut them down and regain the lead. This flushes out the Landlord\'s major threats.\n3.  **Safety**: My pairs are incredibly strong because opponents lack high pairs. I can afford to play the single game now and rely on my pairs to run out cards later after the Rocket clears the way.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠3"]}'}
